  "entityR1": {
    "feedback": "Written before this geometry races. The evidence is as follows. In iteration 4 (0.8 kg, 0.16 m body, 0.10 m track, row at y = 0.03 m), oval oscillation fell to 0.181 and line loss was 0 on all tracks, so restoring inertia cured the jitter that the lighter robot showed in iterations 2 and 3 (0.272 and 0.309). Max_error stayed at 0.0614 m, and it has sat at 0.061 to 0.064 m through every iteration (0 to 4), whatever the track, mass or sensor footprint. Iteration 1 showed that moving the row from y = 0.04 m to y = 0.03 m cut oval oscillation from 0.245 to 0.173, so the reaction point does affect oscillation. For this geometry I kept the iteration 4 controller (kp 2.8, kd 0.35, weights about 33 times x) so that the row is the only change. Because the row now sits closer to the axle, the controller gets less lead, so it may need more damping. Next race: if max_error falls below about 0.06 m and oscillation stays at or below 0.2, the reaction point is the lever, and I will keep it and test the next change. If max_error stays at 0.061 to 0.064 m while oscillation is below 0.2, geometry has little left for max_error, so the controller gain is the next limit, not geometry. If oscillation rises above 0.2 on the oval, the closer row is reacting too sharply. In that case return to y = 0.03 m and try a different lever.",
    "requested_changes": [
      "If max_error stays at 0.061 to 0.064 m and oval steering_oscillation stays at or below 0.2 while oval progress stays below 0.35 (iteration 4 was 0.34): try wheel_radius 0.035 m, keeping the other values, because speed is now the likely limit on progress and the wheel radius is the speed lever.",
      "If oval steering_oscillation rises above 0.2 at y = 0.02 m (iteration 4 at y = 0.03 m was 0.181): return the row to y = 0.03 m, because iteration 1 showed that moving the row from 0.04 m to 0.03 m cut oscillation from 0.245 to 0.173, so a closer row is only useful if the oscillation holds.",
      "If line_loss_events appear on the slalom or s-bend at the outer sensors (iteration 3 showed 0.25 events per run on the slalom at y = 0.03 m, and iteration 4 showed 0): reduce the outer weights to about ±1.33 at x = ±0.06 m, because the outer sensors are reacting too strongly when the line is near the edge of the footprint."
    ]
  },
